def merge_windows(windows, grace):
    # Rule 1: validate before any sorting; report the first inverted window.
    for i, (start, end) in enumerate(windows):
        if end < start:
            raise ValueError(f"inverted window at index {i}")

    # Rule 2: drop zero-length windows silently (they must not bridge others).
    kept = [(start, end) for start, end in windows if end > start]

    # Rule 3: sort by start, then merge when start <= current_end + grace.
    kept.sort(key=lambda w: w[0])

    merged = []
    for start, end in kept:
        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1] + grace:
            # Rule 4: grace never extends; take the largest end absorbed.
            if end > merged[-1][1]:
                merged[-1][1] = end
        else:
            merged.append([start, end])

    # Rule 5: fresh list of [start, end] pairs; input untouched.
    return merged
```

Validation runs first so the reported index matches the original list, zero-length windows are filtered before sorting so they can't bridge a gap, and merging only ever widens the end to the largest absorbed value. Sorting dominates at O(n log n) time, with O(n) extra space for the filtered list and output.

